description:
Scenario: a CA issues a non-conformant cert with an otherName(SmtpUTF8Mailbox) SAN carrying an ASCII mailbox like 'user@example.com' (RFC 8398 §4 says they MUST NOT, but it happens). A caller verifies an ASCII MailboxName("user@example.com") target against this cert.

Expected per pkix-identity/src/lib.rs:455-460:
  /// An internationalized [`MailboxName`] (non-ASCII local-part) can only
  /// match an `otherName(SmtpUTF8Mailbox)` SAN entry — `rfc822Name` is
  /// IA5String and cannot carry non-ASCII bytes. An ASCII [`MailboxName`]
  /// matches `rfc822Name` only. RFC 8398 §4 requires CAs to use
  /// `SmtpUTF8Mailbox` exclusively for non-ASCII mailboxes, so the two
  /// SAN forms encode disjoint identity spaces.

Actual: san_entry_matches_mailbox (lib.rs:482-495) gates the Rfc822Name branch on `!target.is_internationalized` but does NOT gate the OtherName(SmtpUTF8Mailbox) branch. ASCII targets reach the SmtpUTF8 branch and can match.

This is a doc/code disagreement. Either is defensible standalone (strict = doc; liberal = code), but they must agree before 1.0 because both define publicly observable behavior:

- If doc wins (recommended for security conservatism): add `if target.is_internationalized` guard on the SmtpUTF8 branch. Disjoint identity spaces means an ASCII target failing to match an SmtpUTF8Mailbox SAN forces the CA to issue conformant rfc822Name entries.
- If code wins (liberal): rewrite the rustdoc to say 'an ASCII MailboxName may match either rfc822Name or SmtpUTF8Mailbox' and document why (interop with non-conformant CAs).

This is a 1.0 surface decision — the choice fossilizes. Pick one and document the rationale.
